---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ControlBoundaryOverview from "../components/diagrams/ControlBoundaryOverview.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Workflow Examples | BeaconGuard Regulated AI Operations"
  description="Workflow examples for BeaconGuard control across healthcare operations, financial crime and fraud review, and regulated internal automation using ALLOW and DENY."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Workflow Examples</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard applies one Runtime Control Boundary across healthcare, financial services, and regulated internal automation. Existing systems remain in place while requests to governed AI, APIs, or automated systems are controlled before execution. Runtime permission results are ALLOW or DENY only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Shared control-boundary sequence</h2>
    <p>
      Every industry context uses the same reusable sequence: enterprise application context enters the Runtime Control Boundary (optionally via System Connector), evaluation returns ALLOW or DENY, a Verifiable Decision Record is produced, and only ALLOW may proceed to governed execution.
    </p>
    <ControlBoundaryOverview />
    <p class="muted-copy">
      When required approval is absent, the result is DENY with a reason code such as APPROVAL_REQUIRED. Review obligations do not permit execution and are not a third runtime result.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Industry context panels</h2>
    <p>
      The same control boundary applies in each context. What changes is the source application, required deterministic context, control conditions, governed downstream system, and review stakeholders—not the runtime decision model.
    </p>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--scenarios">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Healthcare</h3>
        <p><strong>Typical source application:</strong> Care operations, privacy-gated assist, or EHR-adjacent workflow UI.</p>
        <p><strong>Required deterministic context:</strong> Role, workflow identity, approved pathway, source-system trust, context tags, freshness.</p>
        <p><strong>Example control conditions:</strong> Pathway approval; privacy approval present when required; trust and freshness within bounds.</p>
        <p><strong>Governed downstream system:</strong> Approved model pathway or operational AI assist.</p>
        <p><strong>Review stakeholders:</strong> Privacy, compliance, clinical governance, security, architecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Financial</h3>
        <p><strong>Typical source application:</strong> AML, KYC, fraud, or dispute analyst workflow.</p>
        <p><strong>Required deterministic context:</strong> Analyst or service identity, case and transaction context, source trust, requested action, freshness.</p>
        <p><strong>Example control conditions:</strong> Role and pathway controls; approval state when required; replay and freshness checks.</p>
        <p><strong>Governed downstream system:</strong> Approved governed analysis or recommendation service.</p>
        <p><strong>Review stakeholders:</strong> Risk, compliance, fraud operations, security, audit.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Regulated Internal Automation</h3>
        <p><strong>Typical source application:</strong> Existing internal agent or automation orchestrator.</p>
        <p><strong>Required deterministic context:</strong> Trusted context for role, environment, requested action, change window, freshness.</p>
        <p><strong>Example control conditions:</strong> Approved automation pathway; environment and change-window bounds; Active Verified Release match.</p>
        <p><strong>Governed downstream system:</strong> Production system or regulated automation target.</p>
        <p><strong>Review stakeholders:</strong> Security, operations, risk, audit.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Decision Evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Each workflow emits Verifiable Decision Records after BeaconGuard decides ALLOW or DENY. Reviewers inspect the decision signal, request metadata, release identity, freshness and replay checks, and any review obligation—without treating review as a permission result.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/proof-evidence">Review Decision Evidence</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/healthcare">Healthcare Use Case</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/fintech">Financial Use Case</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
